# Introduction

In this document, we will explore the fundamentals of Analysis of Variance (ANOVA) and apply it to analyze a scientific article. The analysis will focus on understanding the research question, experimental design, factors and levels, significance level, and the results presented in Figure 3 of the article. Finally, we will try to reproduce the results of Figure 3 using RStudio and verify the assumptions of ANOVA.

#  Fundamentals of ANOVA

## 1.1 ANOVA vs. Linear Regression

ANOVA (Analysis of Variance) and linear regression are both statistical methods used to assess relationships between variables and are based on the same general linear model framework.

**Similarities** - Both evaluate the effect of explanatory variables on a quantitative outcome. - Both rely on similar assumptions (independence, normality, and homogeneity of variance). - Both use hypothesis testing to assess statistical significance.

**Differences** - ANOVA is mainly used to compare the means of different groups defined by categorical variables. - Linear regression is mainly used to model and predict a quantitative outcome from one or more predictors, often continuous. - ANOVA focuses on group differences, whereas regression focuses on estimating relationships and predicting values.

In fact, ANOVA is generally used for comparing group means, while linear regression is used for modeling relationships between variables. Mathematically, ANOVA can be viewed as a special case of linear regression.

## 1.2 Types of ANOVA

ANOVA can be adapted to different experimental designs depending on how participants are assigned to conditions and how measurements are collected. The three main types of ANOVA are between-subjects, within-subjects, and mixed ANOVA.

### Between-Subjects ANOVA or inter-group

A Between-Subjects ANOVA compares the means of **two or more independent groups**. Each participant belongs to only one group.

*Example:* Comparing test scores among students taught with three different teaching methods.

### Within-Subjects ANOVA

A Within-Subjects ANOVA (or repeated-measures ANOVA) compares means measured on the same participants under **different conditions or at different times**.

*Example:* Comparing participants' stress levels before, during, and after an intervention.

### Mixed ANOVA

A Mixed ANOVA combines between-subjects and within-subjects factors in a single analysis.

*Example:* Comparing changes in stress levels over time (within-subject factor) between a treatment group and a control group (between-subject factor).

## 1.3 Key Statistical Concepts

ANOVA evaluates how different factors influence a dependent variable. Its results are mainly interpreted through **main effects** and **interaction** **effects**.

### Main Effect

A main effect is the individual **effect** **of one factor on the dependent variable**, independently of the other factors.

*Example:* A significant main effect of treatment means that the average outcome differs between treatment groups.

### Interaction Effect

An interaction effect occurs when the effect of **one factor** **depends** on the level **of another factor**.

*Example:* An interaction between treatment and time means that the effect of treatment changes over time.

